  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "Nepal floods: Death toll climbs to 1,357 as rescue efforts continue",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/nepal-floods-death-toll-climbs-to-1-357-as-rescue-efforts-continue-126090800851_1.html",
      "description": "The death toll from the devastating flash floods in Nepal rose to 1,357 on Tuesday as more bodies were recovered from the affected areas, authorities said.\nRescue teams continued to search for 5,326 people, while 13,583 people have been rescued so far, the National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Authority (NDRRMA) said on Tuesday.\nThe death toll from the devastating flash floods reached 1,357 after more bodes were recovered from different affected areas. The joint command of security forces have recovered 363 bodies from Chitawan district, 223 from Nawalparasi East, 222 from Nawalparasi West and 197 from Nuwakot.\nSimilarly, 169 bodies were recovered from Rasuwa, 75 from Gorkha, 70 from Dhading and 38 from Tanahu.\nSo far, 102 bodies have been handed over to the family members after identification and fulfilling due legal formalities, according to NDRRMA.\nThe floods, triggered by an ice-rock avalanche near the Nepal-Tibet border, sent the Bhotekoshi River raging downstream,",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-08T13:38:05"
    },
    {
      "title": "Flood-hit Nepal races to rebuild bridges, roads with India, China support",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/flood-hit-nepal-races-to-rebuild-bridges-roads-with-india-china-support-126090800423_1.html",
      "description": "Nepal is racing to restore vital road links severed by devastating flash floods, seeking temporary bridges from India and China as authorities work to reconnect isolated communities in the disaster-hit areas.\nThe death toll from the August 26 disaster stood at 1,356 as of Monday, while 4,994 people remained missing, according to the National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Authority (NDRRMA). A total of 13,396 people have been rescued so far.\nThe floods, triggered by an ice-rock avalanche near the Nepal-Tibet border, sent the Bhotekoshi River raging downstream, washing away homes, vehicles, roads and bridges and damaging hydropower projects across northern and central Nepal.\nNepal has sought temporary bridges from both India and China, which have responded positively.\nChina has already sent two 100-metre Bailey bridges to the Korala border crossing in Mustang, The Kathmandu Post newspaper reported on Tuesday.\nThe Department of Roads has estimated damage to roads and bridges",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-08T07:05:28"
    }
  ]
